43: /**
44:  * This hash table supports updating elements. If the new element has the same size as the old
45:  * element, then the update is done in-place. Otherwise a hole is created at the place of the old
46:  * record, which will eventually be removed by a compaction.
47:  *
48:  * <p>The memory is divided into three areas: - Bucket area: they contain bucket heads: an 8 byte
49:  * pointer to the first link of a linked list in the record area - Record area: this contains the
50:  * actual data in linked list elements. A linked list element starts with an 8 byte pointer to the
51:  * next element, and then the record follows. - Staging area: This is a small, temporary storage
52:  * area for writing updated records. This is needed, because before serializing a record, there is
53:  * no way to know in advance how large will it be. Therefore, we can't serialize directly into the
54:  * record area when we are doing an update, because if it turns out to be larger than the old
55:  * record, then it would override some other record that happens to be after the old one in memory.
56:  * The solution is to serialize to the staging area first, and then copy it to the place of the
57:  * original if it has the same size, otherwise allocate a new linked list element at the end of the
58:  * record area, and mark the old one as abandoned. This creates "holes" in the record area, so
59:  * compactions are eventually needed.
60:  *
61:  * <p>Compaction happens by deleting everything in the bucket area, and then reinserting all
62:  * elements. The reinsertion happens by forgetting the structure (the linked lists) of the record
63:  * area, and reading it sequentially, and inserting all non-abandoned records, starting from the
64:  * beginning of the record area. Note, that insertions never override a record that hasn't been read
65:  * by the reinsertion sweep, because both the insertions and readings happen sequentially in the
66:  * record area, and the insertions obviously never overtake the reading sweep.
67:  *
68:  * <p>Note: we have to abandon the old linked list element even when the updated record has a
69:  * smaller size than the original, because otherwise we wouldn't know where the next record starts
70:  * during a reinsertion sweep.
71:  *
72:  * <p>The number of buckets depends on how large are the records. The serializer might be able to
73:  * tell us this, so in this case, we will calculate the number of buckets upfront, and won't do
74:  * resizes. If the serializer doesn't know the size, then we start with a small number of buckets,
75:  * and do resizes as more elements are inserted than the number of buckets.
76:  *
77:  * <p>The number of memory segments given to the staging area is usually one, because it just needs
78:  * to hold one record.
79:  *
